<section id="cftBranding" class="section-content section-margin article-gallery">
    <article class="gap-v gap-h">
        <h2>Branding</h2>
        <h3>Logo Options</h3>
        <div class="gap-v gap-h gallery-3-col">
            <img src="img/case-studies/cft/cftLogoOption1.svg"
                alt="Logo option for Cards for Today based on the Bicycle Cards packaging">
            <img src="img/case-studies/cft/cftLogoOption2.svg"
                alt="Logo option for Cards for Today based on a star in the letter A in Card">
            <img src="img/case-studies/cft/cftLogoOption3.svg" .svg
                alt="Logo option for Cards for Today based on an Ace in a deck of cards with a flipped over A in Today oriented vertically">
        </div>
        <figure>
            <img src="img/case-studies/cft/cftLogoOption4.svg"
                alt="Logo option for Cards for Today based on an Ace in a deck of cards with a flipped over A in Today oriented horizontally">
            <figcaption>The final logo for Cards for Today</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>After meeting the band members and learning more about them as musicians, medical students, and as people, I
            found that the bands' personality felt solid and traditional, and felt that a chunky slab serif typeface was
            the way to go.I also wanted to stylize the “For” to make the design reminiscent of 1920's packaging.</p>
        <p>Finally, I wanted to call back to a deck of cards, and what better card than the most iconic Ace of Spades!
            With a simple inversion, I flipped the A in “Today.”</p>
        <h3>Color</h3>
        <div class="color-palette-group gap-v gap-h">
            <div class="color-palette padding-tb padding-lr gap-v gap-h">
                <div class="gap-v gap-h">
                    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <circle cx="50%" cy="50%" r="49%" />
                    </svg>
                    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <circle cx="50%" cy="50%" r="49%" />
                    </svg>
                    <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
                        <circle cx="50%" cy="50%" r="49%" />
                    </svg>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p>I chose these colors as they are the same as on a standard deck of playing cards. White as the base card;
            black for spades and clubs; and red for diamonds and hearts. In one of our review calls, I jokingly asked if
            the band members knew about how some decks used to come with a <a
                href="https://deckofshields.com/five-suit-decks/">fifth suit</a>, which they did not. But, we agreed
            that adding a fourth color would just complicate the design further.</p>
